							<hr width="100%" size="1" class="hrcolor" />
							Dragons of Norrath should have been, should be now, shelved until they solve far weightier problems.<br /><br />1) Class Balance - I&#039;ve suggested it on the main EQ boards, but they royally fucked up when they made the berzerker class, they should take the correct step, and remove this class altogether before they even attempt to balance the rest.<br /><br />And Class balance needs to not only balance around groups, and raids, but soloing as well. More on that in it&#039;s own part.<br /><br />But roles need to be more clearly defined, and class abilities made to be a bit more distinct than what it is now. For instance, for some reason (I have some ideas of what, but not gonna get into it now) they chose to reset Rangers back into a melee centric role by allowing Archery to stagnate for a long period of time, yet did nothing to increase melee skills/abilities. Why would you want yet another class jockying for the ass end of the mob along with the 4-6 other classes already crowded in back there. Talk about originality!!!<br /><br />They need to make class roles more dynamic than that.<br /><br />2) Make Single group, and single person quests that are not long assed events that take weeks, sometimes months to complete. Example of a piss poor designed quest would be the Breakdown in Communication Quest. Worst design ever. Made the Epic quests look easy. To much time spent on this type of quest.<br /><br />Use instancing to make solidly challening quests that return quality items at the end as rewards. What LDoN could have been, instead of the fucked up mess that it is. Why not have them be more challenging, and thought provoking. Create an elite series that drops Time+ level of gear as a reward at the end.<br /><br />3) Soloing - some have said that enforced grouping is what made the game great, and I think that is totally the wrong way to look at it.<br /><br />Look closely at the original EQ, the one made by Verant. The classes were much better balanced at that time, but why is that? They permitted soloing, and did not take direct action to break soloing skills/spells/locations.<br /><br />The balance was achieved by some classes having an honest edge to soloing. I think originally Monks were meant to have the avoidance/mitigation they had to be able to solo effectively, but not hold aggro. That was the key that the devs missed. Instead of nerfing Monk&#039;s Mitigation, they should have made it so Monks simply can&#039;t hold aggro. That would have ended Monk&#039;s tanking raid targets, while still allowing them to solo effectively for a longer period of time. I highly doubt with current Mob design that they could solo now regardless of mitigation levels though.<br /><br />But the balance was, about half the classes in the game could solo to some extent in an effective manner. Grouping would still give better returns, but if groups were not available, then you had options other than the Tradeskills gambit that so many love to throw into your face. (If you can&#039;t get a group, do tradeskills!, God I get sick of hearing that.)<br /><br />If they seriously want to restore some of the balance that used to exsist, then they need to retune content, and Character Skills, and abilities to allow the old mainstay classes the abilties to solo once again. The primary old school solo classes were always:<br /><br />Multi Mob Kiters:<br />Wizards<br />Druids<br />Bards <br /><br />Fear Kiters:<br />Necro<br />Shadow Knights<br />Ranger (Can also arrow kite, but no where as effective as fear kiting was)<br /><br />Pet Tankers:<br />Magicians<br />Beastlords<br />Shaman<br /><br />Melee Soloist:<br />Monks<br /><br />So that right there was 10 of your classes that had the honest options to solo. They could also Duo very effectively with many non-solo classes. <br /><br />That left Warriors, Enchanters, Clerics, Rogues, Paladins, Berzerkers (in today&#039;s world anyhow) to make groups, and then add in someone soloing as filler DPS/Utility as needed.<br /><br />This created the holy trinity as much as anything did as well.<br /><br />But it also meant that the majority of the classes had options beyond tradeskills for something to do. <br /><br />It wasn&#039;t until PoP, and the destruction of many soloing abilities, and no new content that allowed for soloing of many types, that enforced grouping became a hot topic, and then you heard from many classes bitching because they couldn&#039;t find groups.<br /><br />That&#039;s because now there were too many people/classes LFG, and not enough of the baseline groups to accept them.<br /><br />And most of the time, the groups to do PoP content early on, needed a real tank, and cleric to be efficient. In Luclin and before, the mobs were not so hard, and I remember many groups doing just fine in all types of content with Druids, or Shaman around as healers, and such. That was the other big change, the huge boosts to Clerics and healing, and Druids, and Shaman getting left behind.<br /><br />There are so many things that have changed in this game, that to sit here, and try to list them all, would take a much longer post. <br /><br />Just sitting here, and thinking about the way the old level 1-60 content in the early game was designed (original game through Luclin,) and implemented, and looking at how it&#039;s been changed since then really explains why EQ has so many problems now.<br /><br />The &quot;Vision&quot; was battered and beaten somewhat when luclin was released, it was put down like a dog when PoP came out, and has had it&#039;s grave pissed on for the last few years as the changes kept rolling in.<br /><br />I understand games have to evolve to survive, but you should never do that at the expense of taking things away from players.
